          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "Migraine is a neurological disorder with headache as its most prominent feature, often accompanied by sensory sensitivity, nausea, and sometimes temporary neurological symptoms. It is generally episodic, meaning it occurs in attacks.\n\nA migraine headache is typically:\n*   One-sided.\n*   Pulsating.\n*   Moderate to severe in intensity.\n*   Worsened by routine physical activity.\n*   Accompanied by nausea, vomiting, or sensitivity to light and sound.\n\nThese attacks can last from several hours to up to three days if left untreated. It's worth noting that not every migraine attack will have all these features, and the headache can be bilateral (on both sides of the head) in some cases (Source: condition-migraine).\n\nIn contrast, most headaches are not dangerous. Migraine and tension-type headaches are common and recognized by their patterns (Source: red-flag-sudden-severe-headache). The key difference lies in the specific characteristics of the pain and the accompanying symptoms that define a migraine attack, making it distinct from an ordinary headache.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
